  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Civale gave them enough to win. Seven strikeouts, two runs in four innings. A starter's line that demanded only basic offensive support. They couldn't deliver it. Three hits and seven strikeouts against a Giants team they needed to chase, trailing from the second inning forward and never once ahead. The bullpen stayed clean. The bats stayed silent.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what happens when the offense refuses to work in tandem with competent pitching. Civale wasn't dominant, but he was functional. The kind of start that wins games when the lineup does its part. They didn't. One run on three hits is not an offensive failure; it's a refusal. Soderstrom went 2-for-4, found one in play when it mattered, and it wasn't enough because nobody else arrived. The pattern hasn't changed: inconsistency at the plate, pitching that should be sufficient, and games that slip away in the margins. They've now lost three straight and fallen a full game back in a division where this kind of night compounds into a season.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tyler Mahle takes the ball tonight with a 6.04 ERA, but Gage Jump is coming off four straight starts where his ERA barely budged above 1.43. A ceiling far above anything San Francisco is expecting to hit.",
